WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.261
<v Jamie>SpaceX just priced its IPO and I'm genuinely curious whether the person buying it knows if they own a rocket or a spreadsheet.

00:00:08.261 --> 00:00:27.538
<v Maya>So retail got a sliver of this thing—we're talking low twenty percent range of the total allocation—while the big funds are walking away with the rest, and the real question is whether Musk just proved he doesn't need Wall Street's permission anymore or whether he's about to get absolutely destroyed the moment the lock-up expires.

00:00:27.538 --> 00:00:45.093
<v Alex>Insiders can start selling in tranches. But your actual point stands: a seventy-five billion dollar company where the founder controls the voting structure and the float is deliberately constrained is a recipe for either the smoothest wealth transfer in history or a flash crash that happens on a Tuesday afternoon.

00:00:45.093 --> 00:00:57.141
<v Jamie>So if Musk can set the terms—the price, the allocation, the timeline—and retail investors are basically getting crumbs, what's the mechanism that makes this work? Why do the institutional buyers accept being locked in?

00:00:57.141 --> 00:01:14.697
<v Maya>Because they think it goes higher and they're betting on the momentum play, not the fundamentals. They're also locked in with him, which means they can't panic-sell the moment there's a bad Starship test or a regulatory hiccup—they're hostages to their own allocation, which is exactly how Musk likes it.

00:01:14.697 --> 00:01:26.745
<v Maya>Oracle just warned that a security bug in its database software hit over one hundred companies, and the kicker is nobody caught it until the hackers literally told the world they were exploiting it.

00:01:26.745 --> 00:01:36.383
<v Alex>So a company whose entire job description is 'make sure your data is locked down' shipped a vulnerability that required criminals to announce it before anyone noticed.

00:01:36.383 --> 00:01:47.054
<v Maya>The irony is so dense you could mine it. A hundred-plus enterprises were walking around with the front door open and Oracle's own monitoring systems were apparently taking a nap.

00:01:47.054 --> 00:01:57.381
<v Jamie>Trump just said a deal to end the Iran war is close after calling off strikes, which means oil markets are now pricing in both conflict and peace simultaneously.

00:01:57.381 --> 00:02:07.708
<v Alex>The real signal is that nobody believes the timeline—if there's a deal, it's not locked until it's signed, and we're one bad statement away from the whole thing evaporating.

00:02:07.708 --> 00:02:16.657
<v Maya>World Cup spending this year is absolutely unhinged—host nations are treating stadium construction like a venture-capital burn rate, just hemorrhaging money to prove they can.

00:02:16.657 --> 00:02:28.361
<v Jamie>SpaceX S P V investors who bought into the private rounds won't actually know what they own until the lock-up period ends and they can finally see the fee structure and payout timeline.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/11/spacex-spv-investors-wont-know-their-true-holdings-until-post-ipo-lock-ups-lift/

00:02:28.361 --> 00:02:41.786
<v Alex>So you've got lower-tier investors who funded the company before the IPO, and now they're trapped in a holding pattern where the terms of their own investment are still opaque—that's not transparency, that's a subscription model for uncertainty.

00:02:41.786 --> 00:02:49.703
<v Maya>Air India crash families have spent a year trying to identify remains—some mixed together, some completely misidentified—and authorities still don't have answers.
  ↳ source: https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/c15y9g2ndkno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss

00:02:49.703 --> 00:02:55.211
<v Jamie>So the forensic process broke down completely, or was it just negligence on the ground?

00:02:55.211 --> 00:03:00.718
<v Maya>SpaceX just handed retail investors a lottery ticket to watch the actual winners cash out.
  ↳ source: https://techcrunch.com/2026/06/11/spacex-spv-investors-wont-know-their-true-holdings-until-post-ipo-lock-ups-lift/

00:03:00.718 --> 00:03:07.259
<v Alex>At least you know what you're holding. Oracle's investors still can't confirm their database isn't a haunted house.

00:03:07.259 --> 00:03:12.078
<v Jamie>This show is for entertainment and education only — nothing here is investment advice.
